    "final_answer": "Now I can clearly see all 21 characters displayed by Wolfram Alpha. Looking at the image:\n\n**The character that looks most like a snowflake is U+22C7 (⋇, DIVISION TIMES)** - located at position 4 in the first row.\n\nThis character has exactly 6 radiating lines emerging from a central point, which is the classic snowflake design with 6-fold symmetry. While U+22C6 (⋆, Star Operator) next to it is a 5-pointed star, the Division Times symbol (⋇) at U+22C7 matches the typical snowflake shape with its six pointed arms radiating outward from the center.\n\n## Answer\n\n**U+22C7 (⋇, DIVISION TIMES)** is the character in Unicode range 8900-8920 (decimal) that looks like a snowflake. It has 6 radiating points/arms emanating from a center, matching the typical snowflake's 6-fold symmetry.\n\nNote: Wolfram Alpha interpreted the query \"unicode 8900 to 8920\" as decimal numbers 8900-8920, which correspond to Unicode code points U+22C4 through U+22D8 (in the Mathematical Operators block).",
